{"kind":"request","text":"Converge the project vocabulary to one term per concept, per the decision Hugh made in conversation on 2026-08-08. The generic noun for a thing in gitseq is event; act names the doing (an actor performs an act; admitted, it becomes an event at one position); statement names the projected reading of an event; request is the unit of work; commitment is the tracked request-promise-report aggregate. Retire task and work item from all prose and conventions. The word note keeps its existing job as the everyday UI label for assert. This is documentation and prose convergence only: no persisted formats change.","body":{"conditions":"1) docs/reference.md gains a short glossary defining event, act, statement, kind, request, promise, report, commitment, ticket, and note, including the one-sentence relation: an actor performs an act; admitted, it becomes an event; the fold reads it back as a statement. 2) task and work item no longer name the unit of work in README.md, SKILL.md, AGENTS.md, docs/, or user-facing UI strings; AGENTS.md step 2 reads Rests-On: \u003crequest-event\u003e; the WorkDrawer unlinked tooltip drops work item. 3) New implementation branches use a prefix other than task/ (request/\u003cslug\u003e unless the implementer records a better choice); existing task/* branches and historical notes/ memos are not rewritten. 4) No persisted or wire format changes: the request kind, workroom/state@0 schema, and projection JSON keys stay as they are; renaming the projection Acts subset (ratify/supersede) is recorded as follow-up only. 5) Docs and tests still pass; the change merges through the standard exact-head review loop.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}